.TH MMMKFILE 1 "2026-07-28" "gpfsug-tools" "GPFS/Spectrum Scale Usergroup Tools"
.SH NAME
mmmkfile \- create a file of a given size on a Spectrum Scale (or POSIX)
filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mmmkfile
.RB [ -n ]
.RB [ -v ]
.I size
.I filename
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mmmkfile
creates
.I filename
and gives it
.I size
bytes of space, in the spirit of the classic
.BR mkfile (8).
By default the blocks are actually allocated (the file is not sparse) using the
platform's native preallocation primitive, so the space is reserved without
writing the data through the normal write path.
.PP
The preallocation call used is chosen at build time:
.BR gpfs_prealloc (3)
when built with
.B make GPFS=1
on a Spectrum Scale node,
.BR posix_fallocate (3)
on Linux, or
.B fcntl(F_PREALLOCATE)
on macOS/BSD.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I size
A number with an optional unit suffix (base 1024):
.BR b " (bytes, the default), " k " (KiB), " m " (MiB), " g " (GiB), " t " (TiB)."
For example
.BR 4096 ", " 512k ", " 100m ", " 2g ", " 1t .
.TP
.B -n
Do not allocate blocks; set the file size only. This produces a sparse file
whose reported size is
.I size
but which consumes (almost) no disk space until written.
.TP
.B -v
Verbose: print the file name and size after creating it.
.TP
.B -h
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and non-zero on any error (invalid arguments, a size too
large for the platform, or a failed open/allocate). A diagnostic is printed to
standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Preallocate a 10 GiB file:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mmmkfile 10g /gpfs/scratch/testfile
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create a 1 TiB sparse file (size set, no space consumed):
.PP
.RS
.EX
mmmkfile -n 1t /gpfs/scratch/sparsefile
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
If
.I filename
already exists it is opened and (pre)allocated in place; existing data is not
truncated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkfile (8),
.BR fallocate (1),
.BR posix_fallocate (3),
.BR truncate (1)
